Editing hub scripts with type checking
======================================

lua-language-server reads the definitions in ptokax-api.lua and gives
completion, signatures and type errors for the hub API. No plugin, no server
to write. Any editor with an LSP client works, not just Neovim.

  ptokax-api.lua        generated definitions, do not edit
  gen-api-defs.lua      regenerates them from scripting-interface.txt
  luarc.json.example    per-directory config for the language server


Setup
-----

Copy the config into the directory you edit scripts in, which is your syncdir
rather than the hub's own tree.

	cp contrib/nvim/luarc.json.example ~/.local/share/dchubs/PXdev/.luarc.json

Then edit workspace.library in it to point at contrib/nvim in this checkout.
Open any .lua file in that directory and the server picks it up.


What it catches
---------------

	Core.SendToAll(123)                      integer given, string wanted
	Core.NoSuchFunction()                    undefined field
	SetMan.tStrings.NotAReal                 undefined field
	Core.Kick(nil)                           nil given, User wanted
	Core.Kick(tUser)                         3 arguments wanted, 1 given

Every setting, permission and boolean id is a real field, so a typo in
SetMan.tNumbers or ProfMan.tPermissions is an error rather than a nil at
runtime.


Event functions need annotating
-------------------------------

The server does not take parameter types from the declared global, so tUser is
unknown until you say so. Two lines per handler:

	--- @param tUser User
	--- @param sData string
	function ChatArrival(tUser, sData)
		Core.SendToAll(tUser.sNick .. sData)
	end

Without them the body still checks, but tUser is any and its fields are not.

<leader>pa writes them for you. Put the cursor on the function line and it
inserts one --- @param per argument, typed by the same prefix rule the
generator uses.


Regenerating
------------

	lua contrib/nvim/gen-api-defs.lua scripting.docs/scripting-interface.txt \
	    > contrib/nvim/ptokax-api.lua

Parameter types come from the Hungarian prefix on each name: s string, i and u
integer, b boolean, n number, f function, t table. tUser maps to the User
class. Names the rule cannot type are in the OVERRIDE table near the top.

Return types come from the RETURNS table, keyed Namespace.Function. Anything
not listed falls back to reading the description, which gives boolean? for
"Return nil when failed, true when success", table for "Return table with",
and any otherwise. Add to RETURNS rather than improving the prose reader.

The four tables the docs describe in prose rather than as field lists are
hand-written in PREAMBLE: BotInfo, ScriptInfo, OpChatInfo, HubBotInfo.


Known gaps
----------

Overloads are emitted in document order, so the first form listed is the one
signature help shows first. Core.GetOnlineUsers has four. They all resolve,
the ordering is cosmetic.

Functions taking sNick/sIP alternatives are typed from the first name only,
so BanMan.GetBan is string either way, which happens to be right, but the
pattern is not general.

Nil checking is strict on field access, so Core.GetUser("bob").sNick is an
error until you test the result. It is lenient about passing a string? into a
string parameter, which is lua-language-server behaviour rather than a gap in
the definitions.
